    rich_hr_desc: "Resting heart rate measures the number of times your heart beats per minute when calm. It is a fundamental indicator of cardiovascular health.",
    rich_hr_why: "A healthy heart beats less often because it pumps more blood per beat. Athletes often have lower resting HR.",
    rich_hr_tip: "Always measure at rest, in the morning upon waking. Avoid caffeine beforehand. A consistently low HR is a sign of good fitness.",
    rich_hr_src: "WHO, American Heart Association",
    rich_hr_r1: 'Bradycardia', rich_hr_r2: 'Normal', rich_hr_r3: 'Elevated', rich_hr_r4: 'Tachycardia',

    rich_spo2_desc: "Oxygen saturation (SpO2) indicates the percentage of hemoglobin carrying oxygen in your blood. It reflects lung efficiency.",
    rich_spo2_why: "Your organs need a constant supply of oxygen. Low SpO2 may indicate respiratory or cardiac problems.",
    rich_spo2_tip: "At altitude, SpO2 drops naturally. If it falls below 92% at rest at low altitude, consult quickly.",
    rich_spo2_src: "Pulse Oximetry Guidelines, WHO",
    rich_spo2_r1: 'Critical', rich_spo2_r2: 'Low', rich_spo2_r3: 'Normal',

    rich_bp_desc: "Blood pressure measures the force of blood on artery walls. Systolic (high) corresponds to heart contraction, diastolic (low) to rest.",
    rich_bp_why: "Untreated hypertension silently damages arteries, heart, kidneys and brain over years.",
    rich_bp_tip: "Measure calmly, seated for 5 min. Pressure varies during the day — higher in the morning. Reduce salt and stress.",
    rich_bp_src: "ESC/ESH Guidelines 2023",
    rich_bp_r1: 'Low', rich_bp_r2: 'Optimal', rich_bp_r3: 'Elevated', rich_bp_r4: 'Hypertension',

    rich_temp_desc: "Body temperature naturally fluctuates throughout the day. Lower in the morning (~36.2) and peaks in the afternoon (~37.0).",
    rich_temp_why: "Fever is an immune response. Tracking temperature helps detect infections and inflammations early.",
    rich_temp_tip: "After exercise, temperature rises naturally. Wait 30 min before measuring.",
    rich_temp_src: "Internal Medicine, Harrison's",
    rich_temp_r1: 'Hypothermia', rich_temp_r2: 'Normal', rich_temp_r3: 'Low-grade fever', rich_temp_r4: 'Fever',

    rich_steps_desc: "Daily step count is the best simple indicator of physical activity level. WHO recommends 150 min of moderate activity per week, about 7,000-8,000 steps/day.",
    rich_steps_why: "Walking reduces cardiovascular risk by 31%, diabetes by 33%, and improves mood via endorphin release.",
    rich_steps_tip: "For seniors (65+), 6,000 steps/day is enough to reduce mortality by 50%. Increase by 500 steps per week.",
    rich_steps_src: "JAMA Study 2022, Lee et al.",
    rich_steps_r1: 'Sedentary', rich_steps_r2: 'Somewhat active', rich_steps_r3: 'Active', rich_steps_r4: 'Very active',

    rich_cal_desc: "Calories burned through physical activity (excluding basal metabolism). Varies by intensity, duration, and body weight.",
    rich_cal_why: "Burning calories through activity helps maintain a healthy weight, strengthens the immune system and improves sleep.",
    rich_cal_tip: "30 min of brisk walking = ~150 kcal. Consistency is more important than intensity for long-term health.",
    rich_cal_src: "ACSM Guidelines",
    rich_cal_r1: 'Low', rich_cal_r2: 'Moderate', rich_cal_r3: 'Active',

    rich_dist_desc: "Distance covered during the day reflects your overall mobility. It is an indicator of your autonomy and functional capacity.",
    rich_dist_why: "Maintaining mobility is essential to prevent falls, social isolation, and physical deconditioning.",
    rich_dist_tip: "Vary your routes to stimulate balance and coordination. Walking in nature reduces cortisol by 15%.",
    rich_dist_src: "Journal of Aging & Physical Activity",
    rich_dist_r1: 'Low', rich_dist_r2: 'Moderate', rich_dist_r3: 'Good', rich_dist_r4: 'Excellent',

    rich_hrv_desc: "Heart Rate Variability (HRV) measures beat-to-beat variation. High HRV indicates your nervous system adapts well to stress.",
    rich_hrv_why: "HRV reflects the balance between sympathetic (stress) and parasympathetic (rest) systems. It is a recovery marker.",
    rich_hrv_tip: "HRV is best measured in the morning upon waking. Meditation and deep breathing improve it.",
    rich_hrv_src: "European Society of Cardiology",
    rich_hrv_r1: 'Low', rich_hrv_r2: 'Average', rich_hrv_r3: 'Good', rich_hrv_r4: 'Excellent',

    rich_stress_desc: "Stress level is estimated from HRV, heart rate and skin conductance. It reflects sympathetic nervous system activation.",
    rich_stress_why: "Chronic high stress increases inflammation, blood pressure and risk of depression.",
    rich_stress_tip: "Practice cardiac coherence (5 min, 6 breaths/min) to reduce stress in 3 minutes.",
    rich_stress_src: "HeartMath Institute",
    rich_stress_r1: 'Relaxed', rich_stress_r2: 'Moderate', rich_stress_r3: 'Elevated', rich_stress_r4: 'Very elevated',

    rich_recovery_desc: "Recovery score combines sleep quality, HRV, resting heart rate and stress to assess your exercise capacity.",
    rich_recovery_why: "Training when recovery is low increases injury risk and slows progress.",
    rich_recovery_tip: "If < 50%, favor gentle walking or yoga. Above 80%, you can do intense exercise.",
    rich_recovery_src: "Sports Science, WHOOP Research",
    rich_recovery_r1: 'Low', rich_recovery_r2: 'Moderate', rich_recovery_r3: 'Good', rich_recovery_r4: 'Optimal',

    rich_sleep_desc: "Sleep quality evaluates duration, depth (deep + REM sleep) and continuity of your nights.",
    rich_sleep_why: "Deep sleep repairs tissues and consolidates memory. REM sleep manages emotions and creativity.",
    rich_sleep_tip: "Go to bed and wake up at the same time. Avoid screens 1h before. The room should be cool (18-19 C).",
    rich_sleep_src: "National Sleep Foundation",
    rich_sleep_r1: 'Poor', rich_sleep_r2: 'Average', rich_sleep_r3: 'Good', rich_sleep_r4: 'Excellent',

    rich_weight_desc: "Body weight alone is not enough to assess health. It must be cross-referenced with body composition (fat, muscle, water).",
    rich_weight_why: "Daily variations (0.5-1.5 kg) are normal and related to hydration, digestion and salt.",
    rich_weight_tip: "Always weigh yourself in the morning, fasting, under the same conditions. Track the trend over 2 weeks, not daily variations.",
    rich_weight_src: "Endocrine Society",

    rich_bmi_desc: "Body Mass Index (BMI) relates your weight to your height. It assesses corpulence and risks associated with overweight or underweight.",
    rich_bmi_why: "A high BMI increases cardiovascular, diabetes and osteoarthritis risks. A low BMI weakens bones and immune system.",
    rich_bmi_tip: "BMI does not distinguish fat from muscle mass. Supplement with body composition measurement.",
    rich_bmi_src: "WHO, HAS",
    rich_bmi_r1: 'Underweight', rich_bmi_r2: 'Normal', rich_bmi_r3: 'Overweight', rich_bmi_r4: 'Obesity',

